======================================================================
CRITICAL FINDINGS
======================================================================

1. ROUNDING TIE-BREAKING:
   Integer rounding (round half up) differs from Python's banker's rounding
   This is EXPECTED and CORRECT for FHE (deterministic, no IEEE 754)

2. NOISE BOUNDARY:
   Decryption fails when noise ≥ q/(2t) (theoretical limit)
   K-Elimination doesn't change this - it's a fundamental constraint
   Bootstrap must trigger BEFORE this threshold

3. PERFORMANCE SCALING:
   K-Elimination maintains O(log M) complexity even at 1024-bit moduli
   This enables practical bootstrapping at any security level

4. COPRIMALITY IS NON-NEGOTIABLE:
   K-Elimination REQUIRES gcd(M, A) = 1
   Violation causes modular inverse to fail (expected behavior)
   Parameter generation must enforce coprimality

======================================================================
CONCLUSION: K-Elimination bootstrapping is mathematically sound
            with well-defined limits and edge case behavior.
